# A lemon cell: voltage is not the same thing as useful power
#
# Zinc supplies electrons. Copper is an inert surface where hydrogen ions can
# accept them. The lemon juice carries charge between the two, but its pH alone
# cannot tell us the current, power or lifetime of a real fruit battery.
register lv2
add v1 lemon_juice 50mL
add v1 Zn 1g
new beaker
add v2 lemon_juice 50mL
add v2 Cu 1g

# The voltmeter draws no current. Because this setup has no measured zinc-ion
# activity, the bench labels its voltage as a unit-activity teaching estimate.
cell v1 v2
inspect v1
inspect v2

# Boundary: fruit geometry, electrode area and spacing, polarisation and
# internal resistance decide the loaded voltage and power. None is inferred
# from the open-circuit estimate, and the copper is not treated as Cu/Cu2+.
